      "body": "## Purpose\n\nFixes #56717.\n\n`cmake_build_ext.compute_num_jobs()` sizes the build's parallel job count off\nthe host's CPU core count only (`os.sched_getaffinity(0)` / `os.cpu_count()`)\nwhenever `MAX_JOBS` is unset. It does not check available memory.\n\nCompiling vLLM's CUDA/C++ extensions can use >1-2GB per `cc1plus`/`nvcc` job.\nOn hosts with a high core-count-to-RAM ratio (e.g. a 16-thread laptop CPU\nwith 16GB RAM), the unthrottled default spawns more concurrent jobs than\nmemory can support, causing heavy swapping, OOM kills, or a system freeze\nduring source builds.\n\n## Fix\n\nAdd a stdlib-only `_available_memory_bytes()` helper using `os.sysconf`\n(`SC_AVPHYS_PAGES` * `SC_PAGE_SIZE`), and use it in `compute_num_jobs()` to\ncap `num_jobs` by available memory (~2GB/job estimate) whenever `MAX_JOBS`\nis not explicitly set. An explicit `MAX_JOBS` override is never\nsecond-guessed. Platforms that don't expose these sysconf names (e.g.\nmacOS) get `None` back and fall through to the existing unthrottled\nbehavior, so there's no change for non-Linux hosts.\n\n## Duplicate-work check\n\nSearched `gh issue list` / `gh pr list` for `MAX_JOBS`, `compute_num_jobs`,\nand build-parallelism/OOM terms. #45188 addresses an unrelated `MAX_JOBS`\ninvalid-literal parsing error; no open issue or PR implements\nmemory-aware job throttling.\n\n## Test plan\n\nVerified the throttling logic with standalone simulation (this machine is\nmacOS, where `SC_AVPHYS_PAGES` is unsupported, so the memory-aware branch\nitself falls back to a no-op here and I could not exercise it end-to-end\nvia an actual source build):\n\n- 16 cores, 16GB available -> capped to 8 jobs (matches the reported\nlaptop OOM scenario)\n- 16 cores, 128GB available -> unaffected (16 jobs)\n- 16 cores, memory unknown (e.g. macOS) -> unaffected (16 jobs, existing\nbehavior preserved)\n- 16 cores, 500MB available -> floors at 1 job, never 0\n\nRan `pre-commit run --files setup.py` (ruff check, ruff format, mypy,\nSPDX headers, etc.) - all pass.\n\nI was not able to run an actual memory-constrained Linux build to confirm\nthe throttling fires correctly under `os.sysconf`; would appreciate a\nLinux-based reviewer confirming behavior on real hardware before merge.\n\n## AI assistance disclosure\n\nThis change was developed with AI assistance (Claude Code). I reviewed  \nevery changed line and ran the tests/checks described above myself.",
